Chapter: AccpMcpCard

Section: Instructions

  mc-instructions : Text
  mc-instructions = "Use codex_run for exact arithmetic, counting, parsing and checks of supplied text, and for numerical math and physics. Compute from supplied inputs. Numerical Real results are approximate, not exact proofs. The source language is Codex, not Python or JavaScript. Read codex://accp/language for syntax and codex://accp/science for the optional math and physics libraries. Read codex://accp/experiments for checked quantities, coupled ODEs, sweeps and plots with library experiment. Inspect MathError, scientific result status and convergence as well as execution status. Correct source from compiler diagnostics and retry when a fix is identified. Report refusal, timeout or remaining diagnostics explicitly. Pure computation and buffered Console are available; never widen authority."

  mc-language-card : Text
  mc-language-card = "# Codex computation card\n\nSend a complete compilation unit as source to codex_run. This language is the Cobblestone project's Codex.\n\nChapter: Answer\nSection: Entry\n  opening : [Console] Nothing = act\n    print-line-uni (show (6 * 7))\n  end\nPage 1\n\nNot Codex: `do` (use act ... end), `++` (use &), `main` (use opening), f(x, y) (use f x y).\n\nCode starts with two spaces. Functions use curried application: count-of s want 0 0, not count-of(s, want, 0, 0). A function type lists argument types separated by commas and then -> ReturnType. Function definitions parenthesize each parameter. Use if condition then value else value. Use self recursion for repeated work. Internal text is CCE: derive character codes from literals, never ASCII numbers. text-length and char-code-at count/index encoded bytes; the following example is for ASCII text.\n\nChapter: Count\nSection: Entry\n  count-of : Text, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer\n  count-of (s) (want) (i) (acc) =\n    if i >= text-length s then acc\n    else if char-code-at s i == want then count-of s want (i + 1) (acc + 1)\n    else count-of s want (i + 1) acc\n  opening : [Console] Nothing = act\n    print-line-uni (show (count-of \"strawberry\" (char-code-at \"r\" 0) 0 0))\n  end\nPage 1\n\nThe output is 3 followed by newline. Plain opening : Integer = 6 * 7 also prints its result. Text concatenation uses &, equality uses ==, and Boolean values are True and False. print-line-uni converts internal text to output bytes. Do not use print-line-raw for readable output.\n\nThe service accepts plain Integer, Text, Boolean, Char or Nothing entry results. With library none (the default), source units include their own definitions. Optional library math or physics supplies bundled definitions; read codex://accp/science before use. Arbitrary external cites are not resolved. Source and input are at most 64 KiB each. Only pure and buffered Console effects run. Limits default to 2000 ms and 65536 captured output bytes; ms may be lowered or raised up to the host ceiling of 10000. A lease cannot be requested.\n\nRead status before using stdout. Diagnostics identify source errors to correct. Refused, trapped, timeout and error are failures, never computed answers. codex_check reports a valid entry's row without executing. codex_lens emits WASM text. codex_describe identifies the actual compiler and host; artifact digests do not claim parity or attestation. Keep the returned fact hash when reporting a measured result."

  mc-science-card : Text
  mc-science-card = "# Numerical math and physics\n\nPass library: math or library: physics to codex_run, codex_check or codex_lens. Physics includes math. Omit library or use none for standalone programs. Libraries are bundled Codex source with hashes returned in libraries; compiled_source_sha256 identifies the exact combined unit. Combined UTF-8 source must fit 65536 bytes. The execution limits and ambient effects are unchanged. Arbitrary file or network imports are unavailable.\n\nReal means IEEE binary64. Results are approximations; successful execution does not certify a model, measurement, convergence or error bound. math-format uses the runtime show formatter, which can round away small errors; compare in Real or print real-to-bits for exact binary evidence. Integer arithmetic remains bounded, not arbitrary precision. No symbolic algebra or symbolic calculus is provided.\n\nEvery public calculation below returns MathResult = MathValue (Real) | MathError (Text). Match both arms. math-format displays a value or error: followed by a reason. An execution status of ok can accompany a MathError: the program ran and reported a mathematical failure. Do not report that failure as a numerical answer. Finite checks reject NaN/Infinity; underflow and finite rounding remain possible.\n\nChapter: Answer\n  cites Accp chapter AccpMath\nSection: Functions\n  cubic : Real -> Real\n  cubic (x) = x * x * x\nSection: Entry\n  opening : [Console] Nothing = act\n    print-line-uni (math-format (math-integral cubic 0.0 2.0 100))\n  end\nPage 1\n\nRun that unit with library: math. Only the documented math-* and physics-* functions below form the supported science API. Other bundled dependency names are implementation details.\n\n## Math\n\nmath-pi : Real (rounded constant). math-finite : Real -> Boolean. math-format : MathResult -> Text. math-result : Real -> MathResult checks finiteness.\n\nmath-sqrt x: finite x >= 0.\nmath-sin x, math-cos x: radians, finite x in [-100,100]; larger arguments are refused because argument reduction loses precision.\nmath-atan2 y x: finite coordinates, origin refused; signed-zero distinctions are not preserved.\nmath-pow x n: finite Real base, Integer exponent in [-1000000,1000000]; zero with nonpositive exponent refused.\nmath-exp x: finite x in [-700,700].\nmath-log x: natural logarithm, positive finite x.\n\nmath-derivative f x h: central difference, f : Real -> Real, finite x and positive finite h. Caller chooses h; compare several decreasing steps because cancellation can dominate.\nmath-integral f a b steps: composite Simpson, f : Real -> Real, finite endpoints, even Integer steps in [2,10000]. Reversed endpoints are supported. No automatic error estimate; repeat with finer steps and check stability. Discontinuous or singular functions need separate analysis.\nmath-root f a b tolerance iterations: bisection with f : Real -> Real; finite a < b and opposite endpoint signs or an endpoint root, positive finite absolute x tolerance, Integer iterations in [1,1000]. Continuity is a caller precondition. Nonconvergence is MathError, never a last iterate presented as success.\nmath-ode f t0 y0 t1 steps: scalar classical Runge-Kutta 4, f : Real, Real -> Real represents dy/dt, returns y(t1), Integer steps in [1,10000]. Initial data must be finite. Fixed steps, no stiffness detection or error bound; compare step refinements.\n\n## Physics\n\nUse library: physics and cites Accp chapter AccpPhysics. All arguments are Real; results are MathResult. Units are an explicit SI convention, not type-enforced dimensional analysis. Supply measured parameters and physical constants explicitly. State assumptions and input uncertainty in the answer.\n\nphysics-position x0 velocity acceleration seconds: metres, m/s, m/s^2, s -> metres; constant acceleration, nonnegative elapsed time.\nphysics-force mass acceleration: kg, m/s^2 -> newtons; nonnegative mass.\nphysics-kinetic-energy mass speed: kg, m/s -> joules; classical nonrelativistic motion, nonnegative mass/speed.\nphysics-gravity-force G mass-a mass-b distance: G in m^3/(kg s^2), kg, kg, m -> newtons; point masses, positive G/distance, nonnegative masses.\nphysics-orbital-speed mu radius: gravitational parameter m^3/s^2, metres -> m/s; positive inputs, circular orbit about a central body.\nphysics-projectile-range speed angle gravity: m/s, radians, m/s^2 -> metres; same launch/landing height, uniform gravity, no drag, nonnegative speed, angle in [0,pi/2], positive gravity.\nphysics-spring-period mass stiffness: kg, N/m -> seconds; ideal undamped linear spring, positive inputs.\nphysics-wave-speed frequency wavelength: Hz, metres -> m/s; positive inputs.\nphysics-ohm-current voltage resistance: volts, ohms -> amperes; finite voltage, positive resistance, ideal ohmic element.\nphysics-heat mass specific-heat temperature-change: kg, J/(kg K), K -> joules; nonnegative mass, positive specific heat, constant specific heat, no phase change.\n\nChapter: Projectile\n  cites Accp chapter AccpPhysics\nSection: Entry\n  opening : [Console] Nothing = act\n    print-line-uni (math-format (physics-projectile-range 20.0 (math-pi / 4.0) 9.81))\n  end\nPage 1\n\nRun that unit with library: physics. The specified 9.81 m/s^2 is an assumption for that example, not a universal local gravity measurement."

Page 1
